From e8ab66d4d7d67fda7cc094e7c1e1c6e1687d783b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 11:10:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] Desktop notify receipts carry a delivery_obligation
 that survives a backend crash

A Desktop chat's terminal(background=true, notify=true) job finishes in the Desktop backend that hosts
the chat. _move_to_finished records the result receipt and drops the job's checkpoint entry before
the chat's poller turns the completion into a notice turn. If that backend then dies uncleanly
(SIGKILL, OOM: no orderly exit journal), the in-memory completion went with it and nothing re-delivered
it: recovery reads a receipt as "observed" (result_recorded), and the receipt was retrieval-only for a
tui-owned job (process_registry_obligations.owes_gateway_delivery covers the messaging gateway only).
No incident has been shown to hit this window; this is the defense-in-depth follow-up of
t_01a1d42977b9b3ef (PR #2054, which fixed the proc_70810e2dae05 root cause).

Reuses the PR #1675 obligation/receipt machinery, no second ledger:

- tools/process_registry_obligations.py: a Desktop-owned backend (is_desktop_owned_backend) writes
  the delivery_obligation for a bare-session-key notify job in the same atomic receipt write, before
  the checkpoint entry goes. Every new block carries an owner marker ("gateway" | "tui"; a block
  without one is a gateway's, as before). Recovery re-arms only its own owner's obligations, so a
  Desktop backend never adopts a gateway obligation and a gateway start never adopts a Desktop one.
  recover_from_checkpoint(owner="tui") (startup recovery and the 30 s checkpoint keeper) re-arms an
  owed tui receipt of its own profile whose writer is gone, once, through the re-adopt sink: it parks
  for the chat and wakes the chat when it is not loaded. A tui obligation this process wrote is never
  re-armed by itself (it still holds it in memory). Bounded by OBLIGATION_MAX_AGE_SECONDS /
  OBLIGATION_MAX_RECOVERIES like the gateway's. Gateway behaviour is unchanged.
- tui_gateway/completion_obligation.py (new topical sibling; session_notifications.py stays at its
  2000-line cap): settles the obligation on durable delivery into the session (the notice turn's
  start commit in start_synthesized_turn), on intended suppression (background notifications off, no
  notice text; wait/log/kill already settle in the registry), and when the orderly-exit journal hands
  the notice to the turn marker (exit_job_receipts.journal_session_jobs: a custody transfer, so an
  orderly restart does not deliver it twice).

Tests (tests/tui_gateway/test_tui_completion_obligation.py, real registry, receipts, checkpoint,
re-adopt sink, poller dispatch and start commit; only the worker start is stubbed):
1. a Desktop notify job exits and settles, the backend dies without the journal, the next Desktop
   backend delivers the notice once with exit code 3 and the output;
2. two more restarts after the ack: no duplicate;
3. non-notify, CLI/worker-process, other-profile and gateway-owned jobs do not wake, and a gateway
   start does not adopt a Desktop obligation; a live writer backend keeps its own;
4. wait / read_log / kill / notifications-off settle it, so nothing is re-delivered;
5. an orderly restart through the existing journal (finished notice, or a running job the exit stops)
   delivers exactly once: the journal settles the obligation.

RED on origin/main 91ff285c0a: 9 of 12 failed (no obligation on a tui receipt); the negative
controls not_notify / cli_process / kill passed there by construction. GREEN: 13/13 (the kill case
now settles a just-exited job's owed receipt). Mutant: dropping settle_journaled fails both orderly
exit cases.
